{"meta":{"course":"cat100-supplement","chapter":"02","section":"08","chapter_title":"Resume, Branding, and AI Brainstorming","title":"Module 2 Branding Studio","description":"CAT 100 textbook section connecting module 2 branding studio to privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries, reader notes, support moments, and revision evidence.","learning_objectives":["Analyze how the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.","Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.","Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.","Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it."],"concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming","cat100_supplement_module_2_branding_studio","evidence_based_revision","ai_supported_learning"],"estimated_time_minutes":14,"order":8,"top_tier_hardened":false,"prereq_section_ids":["cat100-supplement/02/07"],"practice_ids":["cat100_supplement_2_8_module_2_branding_studio_artifact_signal","cat100_supplement_2_8_module_2_branding_studio_annotation_signal","cat100_supplement_2_8_module_2_branding_studio_ai_boundary","cat100_supplement_2_8_module_2_branding_studio_calibration"]},"content":"# Module 2 Branding Studio\n\n![Module 2 Branding Studio textbook visual](/course-art/deep-exemplars/cat100-supplement/02-08-module-2-branding-studio.png)\n\n## Why This Section Matters\n\nThis is the capstone of Module 2, where the separate pieces (resume bullets, a bio, the two-AI critique workflow, LinkedIn and portfolio alignment, the AI suggestions you rejected, your revision log, and peer feedback) come together into one branding package. The skill the studio tests is the consistency pass: laying your finished artifacts side by side and catching the contradictions a recruiter would catch, before they do. A package can have a strong resume and a strong LinkedIn and still fail, if the two disagree about your graduation date or your title.\n\nIt matters because this is how your materials will actually be read in the world: not one document in isolation, but several at once, by someone deciding whether the story holds together. The deliverable is the assembled package plus an AI-use log, and the test for \"ready\" is not how polished any single piece looks but whether any two pieces agree on the facts that matter.\n\n## Learning Targets\n\n- Analyze how the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries shows a defensible decision in CAT 100.\n- Distinguish artifact polish from evidence-based revision.\n- Document a bounded AI/software support move with accepted and rejected suggestions.\n- Use feedback, support requests, and revision notes to improve the artifact before sharing it.\n\n## Opening Case\n\nJordan finishes the module feeling done: the resume is sharp, the bio reads well, the LinkedIn is filled out, the portfolio is up. But laying all four next to each other for the studio pass reveals trouble. The resume and bio say \"graduating May 2026\"; LinkedIn still says \"Dec 2025\" from an earlier plan. The portfolio lists no title at all, and the LinkedIn headline says vaguely \"data projects\" while everything else points to a specific Tableau dashboard. Each artifact looked finished on its own; together they tell three slightly different stories about the same person.\n\nThe case shows why the capstone is a consistency check rather than a polishing pass. The errors that survive into a final package are usually not within any one document but between documents, exactly the seams a careful recruiter inspects.\n\n## Core Concept\n\nThe studio pass is a **cross-artifact consistency audit**. You build a comparison table with one row per artifact and one column per fact that a reader cross-references: name, title or role, graduation or key dates, and headline accomplishment. Then you scan down each column looking for cells that disagree. Any column with two different values is a contradiction to resolve, and you resolve it by fixing the facts toward the truth, not by making the prettiest version win.\n\nThe discipline is summarized in one principle and one readiness test:\n\n| Fact column | What must be true | Common drift |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Name | One consistent display form | \"Jordan Ellis\" vs \"Jordan E.\" |\n| Title / role | Same across artifacts | \"major\" vs \"student\" vs blank |\n| Dates | Identical graduation/role dates | An old plan left on one platform |\n| Headline proof | The same concrete accomplishment leads | A specific proof on one, vague on another |\n\nThe readiness test is blunt: pick any two of your artifacts and confirm they agree on all four facts. If they do, and your AI-use log explains where a tool helped, the package is ready to submit. Polishing fonts on a package that still has a date mismatch is effort spent on the wrong layer; the consistency of the facts is the layer that decides whether the package earns trust.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Your resume and bio both say ‘graduating May 2026,’ but your LinkedIn still says ‘Dec 2025’ from an old plan. Every document looks polished on its own. Is the package ready?\" explanation=\"The capstone is a cross-artifact audit, not a per-document polish. A date that disagrees between two artifacts is exactly the seam a recruiter inspects. Each piece looking finished does not make the package finished, the contradiction lives BETWEEN documents.\" options='[{\"text\":\"No, two artifacts disagree on graduation date; resolve it toward the truth first\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Yes, each document looks polished on its own\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Yes, dates do not matter to recruiters\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"No, but only because the fonts differ\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\n## Worked Example: A Final Consistency Pass on the Brand Package\n\nThe studio capstone is the whole package: resume, bio, LinkedIn, portfolio, plus an AI-use log. The deliverable skill is the consistency pass that catches contradictions before a recruiter does.\n\n**Step 1. Lay the four facts side by side, one row per artifact.**\n\n| Artifact | Name | Title | Graduation | Headline accomplishment |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Resume | Jordan Ellis | MIS major | May 2026 | Tableau sales dashboard |\n| Bio | Jordan Ellis | MIS student | May 2026 | Tableau sales dashboard |\n| LinkedIn | Jordan E. | MIS major | Dec 2025 | \"data projects\" |\n| Portfolio | Jordan Ellis | (none listed) | (none) | Tableau dashboard |\n\n**Step 2. Circle the contradictions.** LinkedIn says \"Dec 2025\" while everything else says \"May 2026,\" and the LinkedIn headline is vaguer than the dashboard proof used elsewhere. The portfolio lists no title.\n\n**Step 3. Fix the facts, not the fonts.**\n\n```\n- LinkedIn graduation: Dec 2025 -> May 2026 (matches resume/bio)\n- LinkedIn headline: \"data projects\" -> \"MIS student | Built a Tableau sales dashboard\"\n- Portfolio: add title \"MIS major, expected May 2026\"\n- Display name: standardize \"Jordan Ellis\" across all four\n```\n\n**Step 4. Complete the AI-use log row.**\n\n```\nUsed AI to brainstorm 5 LinkedIn headlines. Chose the one matching my real\ndashboard work, rejected 2 that claimed \"expert analyst,\" rewrote in my voice.\n```\n\n**Step 5. Declare readiness by the right test.** The package is ready because any two artifacts now agree on name, title, date, and proof, and the log explains where a tool helped. That, not polish, is the submit signal.\n\n<step-reveal title=\"Run the consistency pass on a four-artifact package\" steps='[{\"prompt\":\"You have a resume, bio, LinkedIn, and portfolio that each look finished. What is the FIRST move of the studio pass?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Pick the prettiest font and apply it to all four\",\"Lay the four facts (name, title, dates, headline proof) side by side, one row per artifact\",\"Submit the package, it looks done\",\"Rewrite the resume from scratch\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Right. The comparison table is the whole point. Contradictions are only visible when you read DOWN each fact column, not while admiring one page at a time.\"},{\"prompt\":\"Scanning the table, LinkedIn says ‘Dec 2025’ while resume and bio say ‘May 2026,’ and LinkedIn’s headline is the vague ‘data projects.’ How do you resolve these?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"Make the prettiest version win\",\"Fix the facts toward the truth: set LinkedIn to May 2026 and lead with the real Tableau dashboard\",\"Delete LinkedIn\",\"Average the two dates\"],\"answerIndex\":1,\"reveal\":\"Yes. Resolve toward truth, not toward whichever looked nicest. Fix facts before fonts.\"},{\"reveal\":\"You also standardize the display name to ‘Jordan Ellis’ everywhere and add the missing portfolio title, then complete the AI-use log row: brainstormed 5 headlines, kept the one matching real work, rejected 2 that claimed ‘expert analyst.’\"},{\"prompt\":\"What is the actual submit signal, the thing that says the package is ready?\",\"predictType\":\"choice\",\"choices\":[\"A feeling of being done\",\"A new, more professional template\",\"Any two artifacts agree on all four facts, and the log explains the tool’s role\",\"Every document is the same length\"],\"answerIndex\":2,\"reveal\":\"Exactly. Finished-looking is not finished. Agreement on the facts plus a truthful AI-use log is the readiness test.\"}]'></step-reveal>\n\nReduced to its order, the studio pass tabulates, scans, resolves, and only then polishes, with the AI-use log closing it out.\n\n<sequence-builder mode=\"order\" items='[\"Tabulate the four facts (name, title, dates, headline proof), one row per artifact\",\"Circle every column that disagrees across artifacts\",\"Resolve each contradiction toward the truth, updating the wrong artifacts before any design polish\",\"Complete the AI-use log, recording where a tool helped and what you rejected\"]'></sequence-builder>\n\n## Decision Rule\n\nRun the studio pass in this order before you declare the package ready.\n\n1. **Tabulate the four facts** (name, title, dates, headline proof) with one row per artifact, so the columns line up for scanning.\n2. **Circle every column that disagrees** and resolve it toward the truth, then update the artifacts that were wrong.\n3. **Fix facts before fonts:** finish all the consistency edits before touching any design polish, so you do not waste styling effort on a draft that still contradicts itself.\n4. **Complete the AI-use log** so any place a tool helped (brainstorming headlines, tightening a bullet) is on the record, with what you rejected.\n\nThe submit signal is the readiness test, not a feeling of doneness: any two artifacts agree on all four facts, and the log explains the tool's role. Until then, the package is finished-looking, not finished.\n\n## Common Misreadings\n\nOne misreading is that finishing each piece equals finishing the package. The whole point of the capstone is that contradictions live between artifacts, not inside them, so a stack of individually strong documents can still fail the audit. Done-per-piece is not done-per-package.\n\nA second misreading is reaching for design polish to signal completeness. A new template or font makes the package feel professional while leaving a graduation-date mismatch untouched, which is the one thing a recruiter would actually flag. Polish is the last layer; the fact audit is the layer that determines whether the package can be trusted.\n\n## Textbook Walkthrough\n\nAudit a four-artifact package. The comparison table shows name as \"Jordan Ellis\" three times and \"Jordan E.\" once; title as \"MIS major,\" \"MIS student,\" \"MIS major,\" and blank; graduation as May 2026 everywhere except LinkedIn's leftover \"Dec 2025\"; and the headline proof as the Tableau dashboard everywhere except LinkedIn's vague \"data projects.\" Scanning the columns surfaces four contradictions at once, none visible while looking at any single document.\n\nResolve toward the truth. Standardize the display name to \"Jordan Ellis\" across all four. Fix LinkedIn's graduation to May 2026 to match the resume and bio. Add the missing portfolio title, \"MIS major, expected May 2026.\" Rewrite the LinkedIn headline from \"data projects\" to \"MIS student building a Tableau sales dashboard,\" so the strongest proof leads everywhere. Then complete the log row: used AI to brainstorm five LinkedIn headlines, chose the one matching the real dashboard work, rejected two that claimed \"expert analyst,\" rewrote the keeper in your own voice. Now any two artifacts agree on name, title, date, and proof, and the log explains the tool's role, which is the readiness standard the studio is built to enforce.\n\n## What Strong Reading Looks Like\n\nA strong reader audits across documents rather than within them, putting the artifacts in one view and reading down each fact column instead of admiring each page in turn. The contradictions that matter are only visible in the comparison, so the comparison is where attention belongs.\n\nA strong reader also recognizes the capstone as the place every prior skill in the module gets used at once: the evidence discipline from the resume section, the AI-rejection judgment, the alignment check, and the revision log all converge here. Treating the studio as a synthesis, not a fresh task, is the sign the module landed.\n\n## Transfer Case\n\nThe cross-artifact consistency audit is how any multi-piece deliverable should be finished. A job application's resume, cover letter, and references must agree on dates and titles; a course project's report, slides, and demo must agree on the headline result; a small organization's website, brochure, and social profiles must agree on its mission and contact details. The four fact columns change with the deliverable, but the move, tabulate the facts, scan for disagreement, resolve toward truth, then polish, is the same final discipline you practiced on your branding package here.\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"cat100_supplement_module_2_branding_studio\", \"evidence_based_revision\", \"ai_supported_learning\"]'></concept-map>\n\n## Artifact Studio\n\nOpen the studio packet: [download the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries packet](/downloads/summer2026/cat100-supplement/02-08-module-2-branding-studio-artifact.md).\n\n<artifact-studio artifact=\"privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries\" course=\"CAT 100\" section=\"02.08\"></artifact-studio>\n\nUse the studio to create a before/after trace. The first version should show your starting interpretation. The revised version should show one meaningful change. The trace should explain why the change happened and why at least one possible suggestion was not accepted.\n\n## Adaptive Support\n\nALGET uses your annotations and artifact trace to choose support:\n\n- If your note is a **question**, ask for a concise explanation tied to **NACE career readiness**.\n- If your note shows **confusion**, compare a weak and strong version of the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries.\n- If your note is an **insight**, transfer the same decision rule to a new audience or setting.\n- If your note makes a **connection**, show where the connection changes the artifact.\n\n<dynamic-scenario prompt=\"You are revising the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries for CAT 100 02.08. A peer comment points to a evidence move issue connected to ISTE digital citizenship. Choose the next revision move and the evidence that would show it helped.\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\nBefore moving on, use the check to separate surface completion from a decision that another reader can inspect, question, and revise.\n\n<interactive-quiz question=\"Which trace would make the revision easiest to review?\" options='[\"The section gives a longer explanation and a more polished visual.\", \"The AI tool generates the artifact without requiring learner revision.\", \"A before-after artifact record linked to the decision you made and the evidence you used.\", \"The learner completes the page and receives a generic score.\"]' correct-index=\"2\" conceptid=\"cat100_supplement_module_2_branding_studio\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## Final Check\n\nBefore you move on, check that the privacy checklist for accounts, data sharing, and AI tool boundaries records audience, evidence source, AI/software support, accepted revision, rejected suggestion, and confidence change.\n\n## References\n\n- NACE career readiness. https://www.naceweb.org/career-readiness/competencies/career-readiness-defined\n- ISTE digital citizenship. https://iste.org/standards/students\n- data-ink and visual evidence. https://www.edwardtufte.com/tufte/books_vdqi\n- WCAG accessibility. https://www.w3.org/TR/WCAG22/\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_8_module_2_branding_studio_artifact_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_2_branding_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_2_branding_studio","cat100_supplement_resume_branding_and_ai_brainstorming"],"stem":"The Module 2 Branding Studio asks you to submit a complete brand package. Which set of artifacts belongs in that package?","options":["A single polished resume PDF and nothing else.","A resume, a professional bio, an aligned LinkedIn headline and About section, a portfolio link, and an AI-use log.","Only screenshots of your LinkedIn profile.","A folder of unrelated class assignments."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The studio integrates the whole module: resume, bio, LinkedIn, portfolio, and the AI-use log that shows your judgment across them. One isolated file is not a package.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_8_one_artifact_is_package","difficulty":"easy"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_8_module_2_branding_studio_annotation_signal","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_2_branding_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_2_branding_studio","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"When you do the final consistency pass on your brand package, which mismatch is most important to fix?","options":["Your resume uses Arial but your bio uses Calibri.","Your resume lists \"May 2026 graduation\" but your LinkedIn says \"Dec 2025,\" so the two contradict each other.","Your portfolio uses a blue accent and your resume uses black.","Your bio is one sentence longer than your LinkedIn About."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Contradictory facts (two different graduation dates) break trust the moment a recruiter cross-checks. Font and color differences are cosmetic by comparison.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_8_cosmetic_over_factual","difficulty":"medium"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_8_module_2_branding_studio_ai_boundary","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"ai_supported_learning","concept_ids":["ai_supported_learning","cat100_supplement_module_2_branding_studio"],"stem":"For the studio's AI-use log, which entry best demonstrates the bounded, accountable use this module teaches?","options":["\"AI wrote my whole package; I just submitted it.\"","\"I did not use AI at all, so there is nothing to report.\" (but you did use it)","\"I used AI to brainstorm 5 headline options, chose 1 that matched my real focus, rejected 2 that overstated my experience, and rewrote the rest in my own words.\"","\"AI is unreliable so I ignored every suggestion.\""],"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accountable use means the tool helped within a bounded task while you made and recorded the judgments: what you chose, what you rejected, and why. The log shows your voice on top of the tool's help.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_8_ai_does_the_package","difficulty":"hard"},{"id":"cat100_supplement_2_8_module_2_branding_studio_calibration","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"cat100_supplement_module_2_branding_studio","concept_ids":["cat100_supplement_module_2_branding_studio","evidence_based_revision"],"stem":"What is the strongest sign that your Module 2 package is ready to submit?","options":["Every document is visually polished and free of typos.","A reader can open any two artifacts and find the same name, title, dates, and headline accomplishment, and your AI-use log explains any place a tool helped.","It is the longest package you have ever made.","An AI tool rated it a 10 out of 10."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Readiness in this studio is consistency plus accountability: the artifacts agree on the facts, and the AI-use log makes your judgment visible. Polish and length alone do not prove either.","misconception_id":"cat100_supplement_2_8_polish_means_ready","difficulty":"medium"}]}}